    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p33-inner-outer-theory",
     "replacement": "- Consider $y=(5-6x)^5$. Expanding first would be lengthy.\n- Instead, we consider this a **composite function** $y(u(x))$:\n\n$$\\color{#4f9b63}\\underbrace{\\left(\\color{#ef8b2c}\\overbrace{5-6x}^{\\text{inner}}\\color{#4f9b63}\\right)^5}_{\\text{outer}}$$\nLet $u(x)=5-6x$\n\nThen $y(u)=u^5$\n\n- We call $u(x)$ the **inner function** and $y(u)$ the **outer function**.\n- The inner function is evaluated first: it sits inside the brackets or under the root. The outer function is then applied to its result.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p33-inner-outer-theory",
     "targetFingerprint": "bd2164957ea924bc31900391c7d0b4bd2aa36cee3db10d1349a072ab9bca61f5",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
